            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>When to Use This SOP</h2>
<p>Marketing teams managing overseas ad accounts often struggle with scattered data across Meta, Google, and TikTok platforms. This guide provides a clear, step-by-step workflow for reconciling recharge records, spend reports, and invoices — ideal for teams handling 3+ ad accounts.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Pre-Reconciliation Setup</h2>
<h3>1.1 Define the Reconciliation Period</h3>
<p>Use the <strong>calendar month</strong> (1st to last day) as your standard period. This eliminates ambiguity and aligns with financial reporting cycles.</p>
<h3>1.2 Gather Three Core Data Sources</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Data Type</th>
<th>Source</th>
<th>Format</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Recharge Records</td>
<td>Platform or agency portal</td>
<td>Excel/CSV</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spend Reports</td>
<td>Ad manager dashboards</td>
<td>Excel/CSV</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Invoices</td>
<td>Agency or platform billing</td>
<td>PDF</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>1.3 Prepare Your Tools</h3>
<p>Google Sheets or Excel with:<br />
- VLOOKUP for cross-sheet matching<br />
- Conditional formatting to highlight discrepancies<br />
- Pivot tables for multi-account summaries</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Data Export Procedures</h2>
<h3>Meta (Facebook) Ads</h3>
<ol>
<li>Navigate to <strong>Business Manager → Ad Accounts → Reports</strong></li>
<li>Select date range, include: spend, recharge, balance</li>
<li>Export as <strong>"CSV for Excel"</strong></li>
<li>Download invoices from <strong>Business Settings → Billing → Payments</strong></li>
</ol>
<h3>Google Ads</h3>
<ol>
<li>Go to <strong>Tools &amp; Settings → Billing → Overview</strong></li>
<li>Download <strong>Transactions</strong> and <strong>Invoices</strong> separately</li>
<li>Transactions include all recharges, spend, and refunds</li>
<li>If using a tier-1 agent, request their statement</li>
</ol>
<h3>TikTok Ads</h3>
<ol>
<li>Go to <strong>Toolbox → Billing → Billing History</strong></li>
<li>Download <strong>Account Statements</strong> and <strong>Spend Details</strong></li>
<li>Recharge and spend as separate exports</li>
<li>Apply for invoices via <strong>Billing History → Request Invoice</strong></li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: If recharging through an agency, use their reconciliation statement as the primary recharge record, not just platform data, due to processing delays.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Data整理 and Reconciliation</h2>
<h3>3.1 Standardize Currency</h3>
<p>Most overseas ad accounts bill in USD. Convert all figures to a single currency using the <strong>month-end bank middle rate</strong>.</p>
<h3>3.2 Reconciliation Sequence</h3>
<p>Follow this order to isolate issues efficiently:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Total Recharge</strong>: Platform records vs agency statement</li>
<li><strong>Total Spend</strong>: Dashboard spend vs invoice amount</li>
<li><strong>Balance Carryforward</strong>: Prior balance + recharge - spend = current balance</li>
</ol>
<h3>3.3 Flag Discrepancies</h3>
<p>Apply conditional formatting to auto-highlight when:<br />
- Invoice differs from spend by more than ±2%<br />
- Recharge amount doesn't match actual receipt<br />
- Balance calculation doesn't balance</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Troubleshooting Common Discrepancies</h2>
<h3>Issue 1: Minor Decimal Discrepancies</h3>
<p>Platforms may precision-settle to the cent. When aggregating multiple accounts, cents may not match perfectly. Log these as "exchange differences" in financial records.</p>
<h3>Issue 2: Recharge Not Received</h3>
<p>Check for:<br />
- Payment submitted but not confirmed<br />
- Weekend/holiday bank processing delays<br />
- Agency settlement cycle differences</p>
<p><strong>Action</strong>: Contact agency support with proof of payment. Document all communications until resolved.</p>
<h3>Issue 3: Invoice Delayed</h3>
<p>Some platforms issue invoices 3-5 business days after month-end. Use <strong>actual spend</strong> as the reconciliation baseline; file invoices as attachments when received.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Archive and Documentation</h2>
<p>After reconciliation, organize these for records:</p>
<ul>
<li>Summary reconciliation table (all accounts: recharge, spend, invoice)</li>
<li>Raw data exports from each platform</li>
<li>Original invoice PDFs</li>
<li>Discrepancy explanations if any</li>
</ul>
<p>Name folders using <strong>"YYYY-MM_Platform_Reconciliation"</strong> format for easy financial audits.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Reconciliation period covers full calendar month</li>
<li>[ ] All platform data exported and saved</li>
<li>[ ] Currency standardized across all records</li>
<li>[ ] Recharge, spend, and invoice cross-checked</li>
<li>[ ] Discrepancies documented and followed up</li>
<li>[ ] Records archived to shared drive</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Effective overseas ad reconciliation hinges on <strong>data consistency</strong>. By establishing fixed cycles, standardizing formats, and following systematic checks, teams can reduce monthly reconciliation time from half a day to 1-2 hours. For teams managing 10+ accounts, consider automating with tools or custom scripts to further reduce manual errors.</p>
